WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.131 --> 00:00:07.416
<v Daniel>Right now, across the entire country, there are companies completely overhauling their businesses with artificial intelligence.</v>

2
00:00:07.756 --> 00:00:08.416
<v Maya>Oh, absolutely.</v>

3
00:00:08.577 --> 00:00:14.480
<v Daniel>And their output is just skywriting. I mean, they're doing things in minutes that literally used to take days.</v>

4
00:00:14.661 --> 00:00:14.881
<v Maya>Right.</v>

5
00:00:15.221 --> 00:00:22.746
<v Daniel>And yet, almost nobody is getting fired. But, and here is the catch, nobody is getting hired either.</v>

6
00:00:23.206 --> 00:00:24.447
<v Maya>Yeah, that's the real paradox here.</v>

7
00:00:24.847 --> 00:00:31.822
<v Daniel>We are entering this era of the ghost hound scale-up, where businesses can double in size without adding a single desk.</v>

8
00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:40.842
<v Maya>It's just wild. The traditional math of corporate growth is basically breaking down in real time. And honestly, it's leaving economists completely scratching their heads.</v>

9
00:00:41.082 --> 00:00:51.210
<v Daniel>Which is exactly why we are pulling it apart in today's deep dive. So welcome. We are looking at a brand new technical report published just today, November 23, 2026.</v>

10
00:00:51.671 --> 00:00:52.391
<v Maya>Hot off the presses.</v>

11
00:00:52.411 --> 00:01:04.029
<v Daniel>Right. It's by the Anthropic Institute titled Working with AI by Wilmers, Massinkoff and McCrory. And our mission for you today is to cut through all the relentless AI hype.</v>

12
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:05.331
<v Maya>There is so much of it.</v>

13
00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:11.897
<v Daniel>So much. We want to understand exactly how companies are actually integrating AI on the ground right now.</v>

14
00:01:12.478 --> 00:01:15.361
<v Maya>And this report gives us such a fascinating lens because of how it's built.</v>

15
00:01:15.601 --> 00:01:16.662
<v Daniel>Yeah. Walk us through the setup.</v>

16
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:22.454
<v Maya>So it's qualitative research. The researchers actually conducted 66 face-to-face video interviews.</v>

17
00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:23.695
<v Daniel>Like sit-down conversations?</v>

18
00:01:23.815 --> 00:01:32.542
<v Maya>Exactly. About 25 to 40 minutes each across 58 different U.S. companies. And we're talking a massive range here. Law firms, dentists, hedge funds.</v>

19
00:01:32.802 --> 00:01:34.524
<v Daniel>I think there was a trucking company in there, too.</v>

20
00:01:34.824 --> 00:01:42.670
<v Maya>Yep. Trucking companies, manufacturers, and, of course, AI-native startups. And they talked to everyone from executives down to individual contributors.</v>

21
00:01:43.484 --> 00:01:48.191
<v Daniel>Okay, but we do need to lay out the crucial caveats here, just so you know exactly what you're looking at with this data.</v>

22
00:01:48.391 --> 00:01:49.793
<v Maya>For sure. Transparency is key.</v>

23
00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:58.306
<v Daniel>Right. So first, this is strictly qualitative. The companies were chosen for variety, so they aren't a perfectly representative statistical sample.</v>

24
00:01:58.577 --> 00:02:00.338
<v Maya>Right. It's not a randomized controlled trial.</v>

25
00:02:00.539 --> 00:02:09.946
<v Daniel>Exactly. And the value ratings they discuss, that's all self-reported by the interviewees. Nobody was running rigorous A-B tests to prove the ROI.</v>

26
00:02:10.106 --> 00:02:16.391
<v Maya>Yeah. And they actually used an AI model to automatically classify the interview transcripts, which is, you know, very on brand.</v>

27
00:02:16.751 --> 00:02:25.478
<v Daniel>Very meta. But here is the biggest caveat. The authors work at Anthropic. And every single company studied is an Anthropic customer.</v>

28
00:02:25.719 --> 00:02:27.322
<v Maya>Which is a huge flag.</v>

29
00:02:27.403 --> 00:02:35.379
<v Daniel>Right. So I have to push back here. How should we even view this data? Are we just looking at a tech company grading its own homework?</v>

30
00:02:35.614 --> 00:02:49.818
<v Maya>It is such a valid question. And look, the bias is real, and we absolutely have to keep that in mind. But qualitative data like this is still invaluable because macro level statistics, the spreadsheets, they often miss the actual mechanisms of change.</v>

31
00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:50.938
<v Daniel>They don't tell the whole story.</v>

32
00:02:51.218 --> 00:03:03.842
<v Maya>Exactly. You might see a stock price jump or a job posting just vanish, but pure stats miss the human friction. This qualitative approach lets us spot the patterns in the trenches. It shows us the how and the why of AI adoption.</v>

33
00:03:04.122 --> 00:03:11.205
<v Daniel>Okay, let's unpack this. Because since we know who is using the AI, we really need to look at how they are implementing it.</v>

34
00:03:11.886 --> 00:03:13.826
<v Maya>Right, to see if it's actually working.</v>

35
00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:25.872
<v Daniel>Yeah, and early in the report, they have this chart, figure one. It plots nearly 300 different AI applications, comparing the self-reported value against how much organizational change was actually required to make it work.</v>

36
00:03:26.392 --> 00:03:28.493
<v Maya>And the pattern there is just impossible to ignore.</v>

37
00:03:28.848 --> 00:03:39.899
<v Daniel>Totally. They found that light touch uses, like having the AI do some quick ad hoc analysis or just making a slide deck, they fit really easily into existing structures.</v>

38
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.361
<v Maya>Nobody had to change their job description for that.</v>

39
00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:52.912
<v Daniel>Right. But those uses yielded overwhelmingly low value. To get the high value returns, companies had to fundamentally change their processes or completely redefine human roles.</v>

40
00:03:53.297 --> 00:03:57.118
<v Maya>Which completely shatters the illusion of the quick fix.</v>

41
00:03:57.218 --> 00:04:02.319
<v Daniel>It does. It's like taking a minivan and just slapping a flashy spoiler on the back.</v>

42
00:04:02.339 --> 00:04:03.640
<v Maya>Right. You're not going to win a race with that.</v>

43
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:12.162
<v Daniel>Exactly. Versus taking the time to actually rebuild the entire engine for high performance. Does this match what we've seen in historical tech shifts?</v>

44
00:04:12.736 --> 00:04:23.922
<v Maya>It absolutely does. If we connect this to the bigger picture, there are two background papers that explain this perfectly. First is Paul David's 1990 paper about the transition from steam power to electricity.</v>

45
00:04:24.323 --> 00:04:25.763
<v Daniel>Oh, the dynamo in the computer.</v>

46
00:04:25.923 --> 00:04:37.490
<v Maya>Yes. Back then, they had these old mill buildings powered by one giant steam engine. And when electricity arrived, factory owners just retrofitted the steam engine with a massive electric motor.</v>

47
00:04:37.975 --> 00:04:39.876
<v Daniel>So they didn't change the factory layout at all?</v>

48
00:04:40.096 --> 00:04:48.101
<v Maya>Not one bit. And because of that, it literally took decades for electrification to show any real productivity gains. They were just doing a light touch implementation.</v>

49
00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:49.041
<v Daniel>Wow, decades.</v>

50
00:04:49.182 --> 00:04:55.165
<v Maya>Yeah. The big gains only came when they reorganized everything and put small electric motors on every individual machine.</v>

51
00:04:55.345 --> 00:04:57.266
<v Daniel>So they had to change the workflow itself.</v>

52
00:04:57.406 --> 00:05:02.789
<v Maya>Exactly. And that brings in the second paper, Brynjolfsson's 2021 Productivity J-Curve.</v>

53
00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:03.510
<v Daniel>Right, the J-Curve.</v>

54
00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:09.292
<v Maya>He argues that general purpose technologies like AI require massive intangible investments.</v>

55
00:05:09.553 --> 00:05:10.773
<v Daniel>Like training and restructuring.</v>

56
00:05:10.953 --> 00:05:21.360
<v Maya>Yes. You have to endure this grueling period of reorganization before the measured gains actually appear. So the anthropic report is basically showing the J-curve theory happening right now in real time.</v>

57
00:05:21.781 --> 00:05:31.227
<v Daniel>Which makes total sense. But if high value AI requires this massive, painful reorganization, what exactly does that value look like once they actually achieve it?</v>

58
00:05:31.847 --> 00:05:32.968
<v Maya>That's the million dollar question.</v>

59
00:05:33.619 --> 00:05:38.460
<v Daniel>Because the report highlights something really surprising. The value isn't primarily about cutting costs.</v>

60
00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:39.841
<v Maya>No, it's about more output.</v>

61
00:05:40.321 --> 00:05:48.943
<v Daniel>Right. About half the value comes from just speeding up existing work. And the other half comes from creating much better or even entirely new products.</v>

62
00:05:49.523 --> 00:05:52.784
<v Maya>They are expanding capacity, not just shrinking expenses.</v>

63
00:05:53.024 --> 00:06:02.286
<v Daniel>Exactly. I mean, the examples in the report are wild. There's a dental clinic that didn't just automate their notes. They created an after-hours AI answering service.</v>

64
00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:03.471
<v Maya>Oh, that's brilliant.</v>

65
00:06:03.651 --> 00:06:11.780
<v Daniel>Yeah. So it actually triages emergencies at 2 a.m. Or there's a commercial real estate manager trying to lease empty retail spaces.</v>

66
00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:12.640
<v Maya>What do they do?</v>

67
00:06:13.521 --> 00:06:19.928
<v Daniel>They use AI to instantly mock up what a prospective tenant's actual sandwich shop would look like in that specific empty room.</v>

68
00:06:20.214 --> 00:06:25.876
<v Maya>That is incredible. A custom architectural rendering used to take weeks and cost thousands of dollars. Now it's pennies.</v>

69
00:06:26.197 --> 00:06:30.398
<v Daniel>Exactly. But it begs the question, what do the workers get out of all this?</v>

70
00:06:30.739 --> 00:06:31.819
<v Maya>Oh, the employee angle.</v>

71
00:06:32.119 --> 00:06:43.664
<v Daniel>Yeah, because if output goes through the roof, are people just working 10 times harder? The report says in about a tenth of cases, the value actually goes to the employees as less work or nicer work.</v>

72
00:06:44.004 --> 00:06:45.465
<v Maya>Which management doesn't always love.</v>

73
00:06:45.755 --> 00:06:53.358
<v Daniel>No. There's this hilarious quote from an executive complaining about it. He says, quote, they want to use those 10 hours a week to go walk their dog.</v>

74
00:06:54.138 --> 00:06:56.800
<v Maya>That's pretty funny. But it highlights a massive tension.</v>

75
00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:57.380
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

76
00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:04.423
<v Maya>Yeah. And this connects perfectly to a 2026 paper by Humlam and Vestergaard. They looked at Danish administrative data.</v>

77
00:07:04.603 --> 00:07:05.343
<v Daniel>Okay. What did they find?</v>

78
00:07:05.663 --> 00:07:10.085
<v Maya>They found that about 10% of workers who save time with AI literally just take more breaks.</v>

79
00:07:10.549 --> 00:07:11.189
<v Daniel>I mean, good for them.</v>

80
00:07:11.409 --> 00:07:18.913
<v Maya>Right. But it explains this core dynamic. Executives want maximum output. They want you to build 10 sandwich shop mock-ups instead of one.</v>

81
00:07:19.193 --> 00:07:20.974
<v Daniel>Well, the employee just wants to go home early.</v>

82
00:07:21.094 --> 00:07:24.135
<v Maya>Exactly. It's a natural tension whenever you introduce efficiency tools.</v>

83
00:07:25.356 --> 00:07:37.001
<v Daniel>So if output is going up without costs necessarily being cut, how is this impacting the actual human workforce? Because the employment reality here is fascinating.</v>

84
00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:39.440
<v Maya>It is not what the headlines predicted.</v>

85
00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:48.923
<v Daniel>Not at all. There's a surprising lack of mass layoffs. Across these 58 companies, only two saw automation fully eliminate a job. Just</v>

86
00:07:48.983 --> 00:07:49.643
<v Maya>two. Yeah.</v>

87
00:07:50.464 --> 00:08:01.428
<v Daniel>And 10 were actually hiring more people, mostly in IT and AI implementation. Also, fun fact, five companies pushed out employees simply because they refused to use the AI.</v>

88
00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:04.911
<v Maya>So adoption is becoming a basic condition of employment.</v>

89
00:08:05.011 --> 00:08:12.234
<v Daniel>Basically. But here's where it gets really interesting. Two thirds of the companies reported that they are hiring less.</v>

90
00:08:12.434 --> 00:08:13.554
<v Maya>Right. The hiring freeze.</v>

91
00:08:13.734 --> 00:08:20.537
<v Daniel>Yeah. There's a quote from an interviewee that sums it up perfectly. We may not be firing, but we're not hiring.</v>

92
00:08:21.312 --> 00:08:23.593
<v Maya>That is the defining phrase of this whole transition.</v>

93
00:08:23.813 --> 00:08:31.836
<v Daniel>It really is. And it's driven by a few things. Some of it is just attrition without backfilling. So someone quits and they just don't replace them.</v>

94
00:08:32.417 --> 00:08:34.157
<v Maya>The AI just absorbs the workload.</v>

95
00:08:34.657 --> 00:08:44.341
<v Daniel>Exactly. But mostly, they're just avoiding the hires they normally would have made to scale up. There are startups saying they'd need four times the headcount without AI.</v>

96
00:08:45.182 --> 00:08:47.863
<v Maya>Which is staggering when you think about the macro implications.</v>

97
00:08:48.675 --> 00:08:58.158
<v Daniel>One COO literally said he spends 90% of his operations budget on AI tokens. They're adding capacity by buying compute instead of hiring humans.</v>

98
00:08:58.778 --> 00:09:02.459
<v Maya>They are essentially renting servers instead of paying for health insurance.</v>

99
00:09:03.639 --> 00:09:11.701
<v Daniel>Exactly. But wait. While domestic full-time employees seem safe-ish for now, the offshore workforce is getting wiped out.</v>

100
00:09:12.481 --> 00:09:13.822
<v Maya>The offshore cuts are severe.</v>

101
00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:26.154
<v Daniel>Yeah. Nine companies explicitly cut their offshore contractors. We're talking developers, customer service, legal prep. One finance company dropped over 100 offshore contractors at once.</v>

102
00:09:26.494 --> 00:09:27.694
<v Maya>Just replaced them overnight.</v>

103
00:09:28.074 --> 00:09:33.956
<v Daniel>Yeah. The authors flagged this as a huge early warning for the entire international business services industry.</v>

104
00:09:34.456 --> 00:09:40.258
<v Maya>And this qualitative data totally validates the 2026 Borey paper we covered in a previous Deep Dive.</v>

105
00:09:40.958 --> 00:09:42.939
<v Daniel>Oh, right. The one about agentic AI.</v>

106
00:09:43.379 --> 00:09:55.489
<v Maya>Yes. Bori found that firms highly exposed to agentic AI severely cut their job postings, especially for non-senior roles. But, and this is key, with zero negative effects on current wages.</v>

107
00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:57.670
<v Daniel>So the people already in the building are fine.</v>

108
00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:58.390
<v Maya>Exactly.</v>

109
00:09:59.691 --> 00:10:05.873
<v Daniel>The Anthropic Report is giving us the qualitative why behind Bori's quantitative data.</v>

110
00:10:06.414 --> 00:10:09.775
<v Maya>It's the human story of we're not firing, we're not hiring.</v>

111
00:10:10.308 --> 00:10:14.412
<v Daniel>Which also perfectly illustrates that concept from the Humlum paper, right?</v>

112
00:10:15.032 --> 00:10:23.299
<v Maya>Still waters, rapid currents, yes. The administrative registers show no changes in earnings or hours. The still waters. Everything looks calm on paper.</v>

113
00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:24.380
<v Daniel>But underneath?</v>

114
00:10:25.001 --> 00:10:33.308
<v Maya>Underneath, it's rapid currents. Companies are freezing pipelines, shifting work internally, and slashing offshore contracts. Both realities exist at once.</v>

115
00:10:33.733 --> 00:10:41.716
<v Daniel>Okay, so given these rapid currents, how are managers and employees actually navigating the day-to-day deployment of this stuff?</v>

116
00:10:41.956 --> 00:10:45.337
<v Maya>Well, the report identifies three distinct adoption strategies.</v>

117
00:10:45.757 --> 00:10:51.779
<v Daniel>Yeah, let's break those down because the stories here are wild. Strategy one is top-down automation.</v>

118
00:10:52.239 --> 00:10:54.119
<v Maya>This is when management just forces it.</v>

119
00:10:54.379 --> 00:11:02.302
<v Daniel>Right. Like a law firm mailroom that used to take five minutes a letter, a developer came in and turned the whole department into two API calls.</v>

120
00:11:03.052 --> 00:11:05.734
<v Maya>So efficient, but it creates massive friction.</v>

121
00:11:05.854 --> 00:11:12.760
<v Daniel>Huge friction. There was this one company that asked its expert reviewers to train a document review AI agent.</v>

122
00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:13.700
<v Maya>Oh, I remember this.</v>

123
00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:22.247
<v Daniel>Yeah. An agent meant to cut 90% of their plant hiring. The reviewers pushed back hard. They basically said they were being asked to build their own coffin.</v>

124
00:11:22.805 --> 00:11:26.806
<v Maya>The company actually considered handing out gift cards just to bribe them to do it.</v>

125
00:11:27.026 --> 00:11:34.749
<v Daniel>Which is insane. And then there's the Panopticon effect, right? AI monitoring every single customer service transcript in real time.</v>

126
00:11:35.349 --> 00:11:45.392
<v Maya>It creates this intense culture of surveillance. And those top-down tools often break anyway. In a review, he said vendor tools become obsolete in three months because the tech moves too fast.</v>

127
00:11:45.992 --> 00:11:51.694
<v Daniel>So if top-down is a nightmare, what about strategy two? Bottom-up experimentation.</v>

128
00:11:52.112 --> 00:11:54.113
<v Maya>Just handing the AI to the frontline staff?</v>

129
00:11:54.333 --> 00:12:03.556
<v Daniel>Exactly. This is where we get Mark the paralegal. He figures out how to write these incredible AI prompts, but he calls them his secret sauce and hides them from management.</v>

130
00:12:04.017 --> 00:12:07.438
<v Maya>Because he's terrified they'll fire him if they get his prompts.</v>

131
00:12:07.678 --> 00:12:15.201
<v Daniel>Yep. Or the accountant who vibe-coded an 80-hour 401k audit app over a weekend.</v>

132
00:12:15.881 --> 00:12:17.542
<v Maya>Vibe-coding is such a great term.</v>

133
00:12:18.108 --> 00:12:22.489
<v Daniel>It is. But they're huge dangers here. The report calls them clod grenades.</v>

134
00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:27.771
<v Maya>Oh, right. When someone builds a crucial tool and then quits and nobody else knows how it works.</v>

135
00:12:27.891 --> 00:12:35.053
<v Daniel>Exactly. It just blows up in their faces. There's a brilliant quote. A mediocre engineer with an AI is a menace to society.</v>

136
00:12:35.467 --> 00:12:38.508
<v Maya>Because they generate mountains of complex code they don't even understand.</v>

137
00:12:38.608 --> 00:12:48.553
<v Daniel>Right. And actually, the report notes the real bottleneck wasn't customer pushback or regulation. It was the human time required to just review all this AI output.</v>

138
00:12:49.333 --> 00:12:51.314
<v Maya>So it creates a new kind of busy work.</v>

139
00:12:51.694 --> 00:13:03.640
<v Daniel>Yeah. But it really makes me wonder, is AI fundamentally a tool that empowers the worker, like the vibe coder? Or is it a tool that allows the boss to monitor everything, like the panopticon?</v>

140
00:13:03.962 --> 00:13:10.284
<v Maya>This raises an important question, and it echoes Bloom's famous 2009 working paper, which was later published in 2014.</v>

141
00:13:10.645 --> 00:13:13.025
<v Daniel>Right, about technology and firm organization.</v>

142
00:13:13.166 --> 00:13:19.928
<v Maya>Exactly. Bloom theorized that information technology, like databases, pushes decision-making down to workers. It empowers them.</v>

143
00:13:20.088 --> 00:13:20.528
<v Daniel>Makes sense.</v>

144
00:13:20.889 --> 00:13:25.210
<v Maya>But communication technology, like email networks, centralizes power at the top.</v>

145
00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:26.991
<v Daniel>So which one is AI?</v>

146
00:13:27.489 --> 00:13:37.272
<v Maya>The report shows it depends entirely on how the firm sets it up. If they democratize data, it empowers the frontline. But if they use it to monitor customer service transcripts, it centralizes power.</v>

147
00:13:37.632 --> 00:13:38.792
<v Daniel>It's all about management choice.</v>

148
00:13:39.092 --> 00:13:39.592
<v Maya>Precisely.</v>

149
00:13:39.973 --> 00:13:45.854
<v Daniel>Which brings us to the third strategy, reorganizing around AI. This seems to be the sweet spot.</v>

150
00:13:46.034 --> 00:13:48.275
<v Maya>It is. This is where they broaden roles.</v>

151
00:13:48.976 --> 00:13:58.202
<v Daniel>Right. So instead of a workflow bouncing between five people, one person owns the whole thing. The quote was, two people plus Claude is the most amazing thing.</v>

152
00:13:58.462 --> 00:14:00.523
<v Maya>You eliminate all that coordination friction.</v>

153
00:14:00.943 --> 00:14:11.370
<v Daniel>And they are redeploying people to bottleneck tasks, the things AI just can't do yet. Like Ellen the engineer, whose whole job switched from writing code to making high-level architectural decisions.</v>

154
00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:18.998
<v Maya>They're building shared data infrastructure. It's the modern equivalent of Paul David's unit drive. They are rebuilding the whole factory floor for the new engine.</v>

155
00:14:19.178 --> 00:14:22.781
<v Daniel>They really are? But man, it leaves us with some massive open questions.</v>

156
00:14:22.902 --> 00:14:26.225
<v Maya>Oh, absolutely. We have to synthesize all this uncertainty.</v>

157
00:14:26.906 --> 00:14:27.947
<v Daniel>What's the biggest unknown for you?</v>

158
00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:34.552
<v Maya>Well, is this hiring less dynamic just the first stage of displacement or is it a stable new normal?</v>

159
00:14:34.752 --> 00:14:36.793
<v Daniel>Right. Are we never scaling up human headcount again?</v>

160
00:14:37.133 --> 00:14:44.515
<v Maya>Exactly. Also, who ultimately bears the brunt of these offshore contractor cuts? That's a huge sector of the global economy.</v>

161
00:14:44.755 --> 00:14:46.556
<v Daniel>Entire countries rely on that outsourcing.</v>

162
00:14:46.737 --> 00:14:56.581
<v Maya>Yeah. And finally, does broadening roles truly protect workers or does it just postpone the inevitable cuts? Even the authors caveat that none of this guarantees against job loss.</v>

163
00:14:56.721 --> 00:14:59.582
<v Daniel>Right. We might still need a massive policy response down the line.</v>

164
00:14:59.862 --> 00:15:01.543
<v Maya>We can't just rely on corporate benevolence.</v>

165
00:15:01.983 --> 00:15:13.567
<v Daniel>So what does this all mean for you, the listener? The big takeaway is that because employers are deploying the exact same technology so differently, humans still have real choices about how AI changes our work.</v>

166
00:15:14.088 --> 00:15:15.649
<v Maya>The future isn't predetermined.</v>

167
00:15:15.989 --> 00:15:27.896
<v Daniel>It isn't. But I want to leave you with a final thought to mull over. Right now, companies are saving human jobs by redeploying workers to those bottleneck tasks. The strategy, the interpersonal stuff.</v>

168
00:15:28.156 --> 00:15:29.417
<v Maya>The strictly human domains.</v>

169
00:15:29.917 --> 00:15:36.361
<v Daniel>Right. But as these AI models continue to rapidly improve, what happens when those exact bottlenecks are solved?</v>

170
00:15:36.821 --> 00:15:38.262
<v Maya>That is the terrifying question.</v>

171
